WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.039 --> 00:00:02.938
- Hey everybody it's Gary Vaynerchuk,

00:00:02.938 --> 00:00:06.460
and this is episode 309
of the Ask Gary Vee show,

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:10.627
and I'm excited to have
a wonderful entrepreneur

00:00:10.627 --> 00:00:13.132
to take your questions,
so Instagram and Facebook,

00:00:13.132 --> 00:00:15.977
start throwing in your
phone numbers with maybe

00:00:15.977 --> 00:00:18.231
a hashtag or two or data point of like,

00:00:18.231 --> 00:00:20.675
where your question is going,

00:00:20.675 --> 00:00:22.574
to give Raghav some indicator,

00:00:22.574 --> 00:00:24.681
but obviously as we get into this episode

00:00:24.681 --> 00:00:26.859
more data will be thrown
out, and so you can

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:29.113
add on that, continuously put your numbers

00:00:29.113 --> 00:00:32.826
in there, and we will
pick some for the call.

00:00:32.826 --> 00:00:36.454
But this young lady has built
a very impressive resume

00:00:36.454 --> 00:00:39.267
we met on the Today Show doing
some entrepreneurial stuff

00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:40.817
and I will let Alli say hello.

00:00:40.817 --> 00:00:43.238
So for the VaynerNation,
why don't you tell everybody

00:00:43.238 --> 00:00:45.623
who you are, and what you do.

00:00:45.623 --> 00:00:48.717
- So my name is Alli Webb,
and I founded a company

00:00:48.717 --> 00:00:50.466
called Drybar.

00:00:50.466 --> 00:00:52.120
I feel like there's a
lot of men in this room,

00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:54.009
but for all the ladies out there,

00:00:54.009 --> 00:00:55.748
we are a blow dry bar.

00:00:55.748 --> 00:00:57.550
- [Gary] Actually it's only four to three.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:58.971
This is actually the best
ratio I think we've normally

00:00:58.971 --> 00:01:00.072
have ever had.

00:01:00.072 --> 00:01:01.872
- You're right, you're right.
I'm looking at all the guys.

00:01:01.872 --> 00:01:03.023
(laughing)

00:01:03.023 --> 00:01:05.115
- [Gary] I'm pretty public
to the four to three ratio.

00:01:05.115 --> 00:01:05.948
Go ahead!

00:01:05.948 --> 00:01:07.786
- Good! I'm happy to hear that.

00:01:07.786 --> 00:01:10.212
So yeah, we were the first,
we started ten years ago,

00:01:10.212 --> 00:01:12.979
we have 125 locations across the country,

00:01:12.979 --> 00:01:14.045
four thousand stylists.

00:01:14.045 --> 00:01:15.286
- [Gary] When did you start?

00:01:15.286 --> 00:01:16.208
- 2010.

00:01:16.208 --> 00:01:17.249
- [Gary] And when you
say you were the first,

00:01:17.249 --> 00:01:19.067
because I'm very aware of the movement,

00:01:19.067 --> 00:01:21.833
or maybe it's just because I live in it.

00:01:21.833 --> 00:01:23.286
- There was nothing
like it when I started.

00:01:23.286 --> 00:01:24.119
- [Gary] Nothing.

00:01:24.119 --> 00:01:24.952
- Nothing.

00:01:24.952 --> 00:01:26.160
- [Gary] And so back to that,

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:27.884
and by the way real quick for
all the entrepreneurs there,

00:01:27.884 --> 00:01:30.020
one of the biggest questions I get is,

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:33.009
people take so much emotion in inventing,

00:01:33.009 --> 00:01:35.925
we're adding on to something
a little bit differently,

00:01:35.925 --> 00:01:38.121
and then when there's
something successful,

00:01:38.121 --> 00:01:41.084
there's fast followers, and
they get so upset about that

00:01:41.084 --> 00:01:44.502
and I'm like look, that's
just market dynamics.

00:01:44.502 --> 00:01:46.864
- Yeah. I mean, listen, I feel like,

00:01:46.864 --> 00:01:49.334
to unpack that a little,

00:01:49.334 --> 00:01:50.609
we didn't invent blowouts.

00:01:50.609 --> 00:01:52.634
We just created a much
better experience around it,

00:01:52.634 --> 00:01:53.467
and the advice I give...

00:01:53.467 --> 00:01:54.780
- [Gary] And you went all in.

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:57.082
- And we just kinda changed the game,

00:01:57.082 --> 00:01:59.441
and the advice I give entrepreneurs is

00:01:59.441 --> 00:02:01.758
there may be something
out there that exists

00:02:01.758 --> 00:02:03.729
that you love, you just
wish was done better.

00:02:03.729 --> 00:02:07.245
And we created a much better
experience and price point,

00:02:07.245 --> 00:02:10.184
and location around blowouts
that just did not exist.

00:02:10.184 --> 00:02:13.054
You used to have to go
to a full service salon,

00:02:13.054 --> 00:02:15.959
or a discount chain, and the
experience just wasn't great,

00:02:15.959 --> 00:02:16.792
we were overpaying.

00:02:16.792 --> 00:02:18.252
- [Gary] Am I wrong in this?

00:02:18.252 --> 00:02:21.018
Wasn't it just one of the menu
items versus the core item?

00:02:21.018 --> 00:02:24.155
- It was a menu item, and it
was also one that most stylists

00:02:24.155 --> 00:02:26.446
didn't wanna do because
they wanted to use that hour

00:02:26.446 --> 00:02:28.796
to do a haircut that you
could charge a lot more from,

00:02:28.796 --> 00:02:31.610
they didn't want to do a
blowout which was less money.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:33.633
And it's funny 'cause it
really harkens back to the day

00:02:33.633 --> 00:02:36.025
of like our grandmothers, and
grandmothers generation where

00:02:36.025 --> 00:02:38.098
they get their hair quaffed
and they wouldn't touch it

00:02:38.098 --> 00:02:39.036
for like, a week.

00:02:39.036 --> 00:02:40.838
We kinda modernized that behavior.

00:02:40.838 --> 00:02:43.919
But, make no mistake, I did
not know, none of us knew,

00:02:43.919 --> 00:02:46.858
my brother, Michael Landau's
my business partner, also bald.

00:02:46.858 --> 00:02:50.443
Like, none of us knew
what was in front of us.

00:02:50.443 --> 00:02:52.933
We didn't realize how
big this opportunity was.

00:02:52.933 --> 00:02:55.010
I was gonna open one store in L.A.,

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:56.605
pick up my kids from preschool,

00:02:56.605 --> 00:02:59.206
and that was gonna be my little life.

00:02:59.206 --> 00:03:00.774
The fact that we like,

00:03:00.774 --> 00:03:02.499
we so underestimated the demand.

00:03:02.499 --> 00:03:04.361
- Let me give you a comp,
and then we'll go back

00:03:04.361 --> 00:03:05.846
to the origin story.

00:03:05.846 --> 00:03:08.348
For awhile now, about 20 months,

00:03:08.348 --> 00:03:10.366
it's been running through
my head that like,

00:03:10.366 --> 00:03:14.568
a french fries only
restaurant QSR would dominate.

00:03:14.568 --> 00:03:17.570
You didn't invent french
fries, it was a side dish.

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:19.282
- Could you get like all
different flavors of french fries?

00:03:19.282 --> 00:03:21.728
- Yeah, I'm really hot on
the whole concept of like,

00:03:21.728 --> 00:03:24.066
the different ketchups
and mustards and salts,

00:03:24.066 --> 00:03:25.811
but one core french fry.

00:03:25.811 --> 00:03:27.258
So it's not like seven
different french fries,

00:03:27.258 --> 00:03:30.671
my concept is, this crazy french
fry that everyone's about,

00:03:30.671 --> 00:03:34.669
but it's the other things,
11 different ketchups,

00:03:34.669 --> 00:03:37.682
the 11 different mayos,
the 11 different mustards,

00:03:37.682 --> 00:03:38.589
and like the 13 different salts.

00:03:38.589 --> 00:03:39.422
- [Alli] Interesting.

00:03:39.422 --> 00:03:41.989
Except that french fries
are so bad for you!

00:03:41.989 --> 00:03:43.314
But they're my weakness.

00:03:43.314 --> 00:03:44.147
No but they're my weakness.

00:03:44.147 --> 00:03:44.980
At least we have a healthy addition.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:46.723
- [Gary] Alli, every single
person is getting brain cancer

00:03:46.723 --> 00:03:47.789
from cell phones.

00:03:47.789 --> 00:03:51.422
I don't need to hear people's
hot takes on french fries.

00:03:51.422 --> 00:03:53.921
- [Alli] If that was like
the last thing I could eat,

00:03:53.921 --> 00:03:54.809
it would be french fries.

00:03:54.809 --> 00:03:56.963
I love french fries, so much.

00:03:56.963 --> 00:03:59.865
So I am all in on this business.

00:03:59.865 --> 00:04:01.336
Listen, do one thing and do it well!

00:04:01.336 --> 00:04:03.015
That's my concept.

00:04:03.015 --> 00:04:03.848
- That's right though.

00:04:03.848 --> 00:04:05.076
- [Alli] I know that's
my french fry concept.

00:04:05.076 --> 00:04:07.891
- So you started Drybar, 125 locations?

00:04:07.891 --> 00:04:08.724
- [Alli] Yes.

00:04:08.724 --> 00:04:09.557
- Franchise model?

00:04:09.557 --> 00:04:10.390
Or all yours?

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:12.339
- They're predominately company owned,

00:04:12.339 --> 00:04:15.047
I'd say we're about forty,
give or take, franchised

00:04:15.047 --> 00:04:16.691
and the rest company owned.

00:04:16.691 --> 00:04:20.127
Which you know, I could make
the argument either way,

00:04:20.127 --> 00:04:22.407
there's great things about franchising

00:04:22.407 --> 00:04:25.245
and there's not great
things about franchising.

00:04:25.245 --> 00:04:27.000
Again, I could make the
argument either way.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:29.898
- The reason you could make
an argument is now here we are

00:04:29.898 --> 00:04:33.019
75 years later, and
both independently owned

00:04:33.019 --> 00:04:35.211
corporate entities, and franchise models,

00:04:35.211 --> 00:04:36.765
both have a place at the table.

00:04:36.765 --> 00:04:38.598
- [Alli] They really do.

00:04:38.598 --> 00:04:39.431
- The question is,

00:04:39.431 --> 00:04:41.180
the mix of the two is
always a fascinating thing.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.361
- And it's challenging
because it's a whole business,

00:04:44.361 --> 00:04:46.953
finding and running a
franchise organization,

00:04:46.953 --> 00:04:48.127
and there's a lot,

00:04:48.127 --> 00:04:51.309
it's just a different set
of problems and issues.

00:04:51.309 --> 00:04:54.154
But yeah, I think both are great.

00:04:54.154 --> 00:04:56.393
- Where's the momentum in the company now?

00:04:56.393 --> 00:04:58.543
- Product is a big push.

00:04:58.543 --> 00:05:01.818
I mean we have almost
three thousand points

00:05:01.818 --> 00:05:04.430
of distribution where
Nordstrom, Ulta, Sephora,

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:06.474
we're international with product,

00:05:06.474 --> 00:05:08.617
we have a massive product
line from tools to

00:05:08.617 --> 00:05:11.503
dry shampoos, to shampoos, you name it,

00:05:11.503 --> 00:05:13.903
and what's great is if you
don't have access to Drybar

00:05:13.903 --> 00:05:16.923
but you know and love Drybar,
which luckily most women

00:05:16.923 --> 00:05:19.558
know about now, you can buy the product

00:05:19.558 --> 00:05:22.167
and kinda replicate what
we're doing in the shops.

00:05:22.167 --> 00:05:23.648
- Let's take this way way back.

00:05:23.648 --> 00:05:25.598
What kind of girl were you growing up?

00:05:25.598 --> 00:05:26.800
Were you entrepreneurial?

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:27.991
Were you like, sporty?

00:05:27.991 --> 00:05:29.052
Were you like a nerd?

00:05:29.052 --> 00:05:31.829
- Well, if you ask my parents,
they would have told you

00:05:31.829 --> 00:05:33.734
I was such the underachiever.

00:05:33.734 --> 00:05:36.531
My brother, Michael, who I
mentioned, total overachiever.

00:05:36.531 --> 00:05:40.200
Micheal P. Keaton, which I
know you get that reference.

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.142
- Oh! Got it, like he was.

00:05:41.142 --> 00:05:42.661
- [Alli] Remember from Family Ties?

00:05:42.661 --> 00:05:43.835
- Yes, of course! Michael J. Fox!

00:05:43.835 --> 00:05:46.278
- [Alli] Yes! I'm sure a
lot of people listening

00:05:46.278 --> 00:05:48.451
don't know that reference, but
he was like the overachiever

00:05:48.451 --> 00:05:49.284
of the family.

00:05:49.284 --> 00:05:50.995
I was just, I've always been the...

00:05:50.995 --> 00:05:51.828
- You were Mallory?

00:05:51.828 --> 00:05:53.904
- I was like, I don't
totally remember her.

00:05:53.904 --> 00:05:55.335
I remember her but not her character.

00:05:55.335 --> 00:05:57.438
But I was kinda like,
it'll all work out and

00:05:57.438 --> 00:05:59.226
I was never really worried about things.

00:05:59.226 --> 00:06:00.082
- Where'd you grow up?

00:06:00.082 --> 00:06:00.915
- [Alli] South Florida.

00:06:00.915 --> 00:06:02.696
And, my hair is naturally curly.

00:06:02.696 --> 00:06:04.946
So in South Florida it was very frizzy,

00:06:04.946 --> 00:06:06.335
and I was always trying to figure out

00:06:06.335 --> 00:06:07.620
how to get it straight.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.469
I used to beg my mom to blowout my hair,

00:06:09.469 --> 00:06:12.432
and that theme of hair and
trying to figure out my hair

00:06:12.432 --> 00:06:15.480
kinda was always there and I
feel like the seed was planted

00:06:15.480 --> 00:06:17.587
to do something in hair when I was a kid.

00:06:17.587 --> 00:06:21.433
But then as I grew up and went
to high school and college,

00:06:21.433 --> 00:06:22.723
well, I didn't really go to college

00:06:22.723 --> 00:06:24.436
I went for like a minute,

00:06:24.436 --> 00:06:26.801
all my friends knew what
they were gonna major in

00:06:26.801 --> 00:06:28.171
and I really didn't know

00:06:28.171 --> 00:06:30.422
and I was so lost and
confused out of high school

00:06:30.422 --> 00:06:32.270
and I went to college for a second.

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:34.366
- Like a semester?

00:06:34.366 --> 00:06:35.458
A half a semester?

00:06:35.458 --> 00:06:36.291
- I think I maybe went for a year.

00:06:36.291 --> 00:06:37.124
- 'Cause you're like, my hero.

00:06:37.124 --> 00:06:39.038
- [Alli] I don't think I went
to like class all that much,

00:06:39.038 --> 00:06:41.816
'cause I was like, I'm not
interested in anything.

00:06:41.816 --> 00:06:44.398
- To make you feel comfortable
about this conversation,

00:06:44.398 --> 00:06:47.315
this is my high school report card.

00:06:49.008 --> 00:06:52.242
- Yeah I mean, we're about the same.

00:06:52.242 --> 00:06:54.875
- Just notice that there's
only four A's, all in gym.

00:06:54.875 --> 00:06:56.185
- Here's the greatest thing about

00:06:56.185 --> 00:06:58.269
one of my favorite things about my life

00:06:58.269 --> 00:07:01.892
is that I get asked to speak
at colleges all the time.

00:07:01.892 --> 00:07:03.667
And every time they ask me I'm like,

00:07:03.667 --> 00:07:06.957
you know I didn't actually
graduate from college,

00:07:06.957 --> 00:07:08.415
I just wanna be upfront with that.

00:07:08.415 --> 00:07:09.881
But that just goes to show you that,

00:07:09.881 --> 00:07:10.980
not that I'm against college, I mean,

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:13.285
go to college, get an
education however you can,

00:07:13.285 --> 00:07:14.875
but my parents were entrepreneurs,

00:07:14.875 --> 00:07:16.643
so I grew up in a
entrepreneurial environment.

00:07:16.643 --> 00:07:18.653
So I didn't know I was
getting that education.

00:07:18.653 --> 00:07:21.154
- They didn't frown on...

00:07:21.154 --> 00:07:23.000
- No 'cause my parents
didn't go to college either.

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:25.724
- But a lot of parents go the other way!

00:07:25.724 --> 00:07:28.537
- But my parents were so cool,
chill, and they were like,

00:07:28.537 --> 00:07:30.247
we just want you to do
what makes you happy.

00:07:30.247 --> 00:07:32.694
They weren't so thrilled
when I made the decision

00:07:32.694 --> 00:07:34.662
to go to beauty school.

00:07:34.662 --> 00:07:36.503
- And is that what happened next?

00:07:36.503 --> 00:07:39.786
- No, I went to,
technically, to Florida State

00:07:39.786 --> 00:07:41.357
I just didn't go to class very often

00:07:41.357 --> 00:07:42.808
'cause I grew up in Florida.

00:07:42.808 --> 00:07:44.875
And then, I was like this is not for me,

00:07:44.875 --> 00:07:47.302
I moved home, and then I
moved to New York City,

00:07:47.302 --> 00:07:49.363
I thought I wanted to work in
fashion, and I worked for like

00:07:49.363 --> 00:07:50.879
Nicole Miller and Cynthia Rowley,

00:07:50.879 --> 00:07:52.761
this was like 25 years ago,

00:07:52.761 --> 00:07:55.202
and then my brother and I started

00:07:55.202 --> 00:07:58.369
Nicole Miller licensed stores in Boca,

00:08:00.048 --> 00:08:01.078
which is where we grew up.

00:08:01.078 --> 00:08:02.467
Realized didn't wanna do that,

00:08:02.467 --> 00:08:03.419
and Michael and I were fighting so much,

00:08:03.419 --> 00:08:05.510
and I was like, what the hell
am I gonna do with my life?

00:08:05.510 --> 00:08:06.752
And I was like, you know the only thing

00:08:06.752 --> 00:08:08.754
that I'm really passionate about is hair.

00:08:08.754 --> 00:08:10.125
And I wanna learn how to do hair,

00:08:10.125 --> 00:08:11.921
and I had worked at a
hair salon in high school,

00:08:11.921 --> 00:08:13.451
where they would blow out my hair

00:08:13.451 --> 00:08:14.580
and it was so magical to me.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:17.024
So I said, I think I
should go to beauty school.

00:08:17.024 --> 00:08:19.434
And my brother by the way
was like, you should go,

00:08:19.434 --> 00:08:21.378
I can see you doing
editorial and fashion shows

00:08:21.378 --> 00:08:22.895
in New York and I had this grand vision

00:08:22.895 --> 00:08:25.566
of what I would do with hair,
where my parent's stores

00:08:25.566 --> 00:08:27.569
were like little old lady clothing stores,

00:08:27.569 --> 00:08:29.965
and like, retirement
communities in South Florida,

00:08:29.965 --> 00:08:33.072
and they were like, you wanna
go work at beauty parlors?

00:08:33.072 --> 00:08:36.301
And I was like, no, I wanna
do something amazing in hair!

00:08:36.301 --> 00:08:38.429
Of course I didn't have the
vision of Drybar back then,

00:08:38.429 --> 00:08:40.918
but I took the plunge,
went to beauty school,

00:08:40.918 --> 00:08:43.199
again, to my parent's dismay.

00:08:43.199 --> 00:08:44.548
- [Gary] And how did that go?

00:08:44.548 --> 00:08:46.426
- They supported it and paid for it,

00:08:46.426 --> 00:08:48.516
I feel like my mom was paying
off those loans forever

00:08:48.516 --> 00:08:49.565
from beauty school,

00:08:49.565 --> 00:08:51.429
'cause beauty school wasn't cheap either.

00:08:51.429 --> 00:08:55.398
But literally the day I
walked in, I was like,

00:08:55.398 --> 00:08:57.232
this is where I was meant to be.

00:08:57.232 --> 00:08:59.995
I loved it, I fell in love
with it, I wanted to learn

00:08:59.995 --> 00:09:00.828
everything I could learn.

00:09:00.828 --> 00:09:02.546
It was the only thing I was
ever really passionate about,

00:09:02.546 --> 00:09:06.113
which, again is such a good
example for people out there

00:09:06.113 --> 00:09:09.691
who are like, they feel like
maybe the thing that they're

00:09:09.691 --> 00:09:10.856
passionate about isn't like,

00:09:10.856 --> 00:09:12.725
a great career option, you know?

00:09:12.725 --> 00:09:14.600
- It's my singular thesis.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:17.356
I really believe somebody
watching right now

00:09:17.356 --> 00:09:21.470
who loves Star Trek or
Black Panther or Fortnite,

00:09:21.470 --> 00:09:24.694
I really genuinely, or
making homemade jam,

00:09:24.694 --> 00:09:25.527
I believe it.

00:09:25.527 --> 00:09:27.576
And I believe it because of the internet.

00:09:27.576 --> 00:09:29.317
I didn't believe it in 1963,

00:09:29.317 --> 00:09:31.973
because there wasn't a system to go into,

00:09:31.973 --> 00:09:34.687
like you can start a Black
Panther Instagram account,

00:09:34.687 --> 00:09:38.624
and a podcast, and be active
on Black Panther forms

00:09:38.624 --> 00:09:42.431
and Facebook groups, and
overtime get traction,

00:09:42.431 --> 00:09:44.722
and something could happen.

00:09:44.722 --> 00:09:45.798
That wasn't real.

00:09:45.798 --> 00:09:47.101
- [Alli] We're so living
in the day where like,

00:09:47.101 --> 00:09:49.804
anything can happen, anybody can make it

00:09:49.804 --> 00:09:51.173
doing all sorts of stuff,

00:09:51.173 --> 00:09:52.408
and I think I'm a great example of that.

00:09:52.408 --> 00:09:54.082
- I apologize, but I think making it

00:09:54.082 --> 00:09:55.851
is the biggest conversation
that entrepreneurs like

00:09:55.851 --> 00:09:57.105
you and I who've made it

00:09:57.105 --> 00:09:59.634
need to start helping the next generation,

00:09:59.634 --> 00:10:02.172
because making it, I believe

00:10:02.172 --> 00:10:04.473
if you love Teenage Mutant Ninja Turtles,

00:10:04.473 --> 00:10:06.158
and you make 100 thousand a year,

00:10:06.158 --> 00:10:09.478
because you have four sponsors:
Nickelodeon, the convention,

00:10:09.478 --> 00:10:11.680
who knows, right?

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:13.161
And you make 100 thousand a year

00:10:13.161 --> 00:10:17.612
hosting a TMNT, Teenage
Mutant Ninja Turtles Podcast

00:10:17.612 --> 00:10:21.460
and Instagram account, is
a much better life than

00:10:21.460 --> 00:10:24.914
making 213k as an executive at Mckinsey

00:10:24.914 --> 00:10:28.354
and being grossly unhappy,
and taking sleeping pills.

00:10:28.354 --> 00:10:32.026
- Right! Because I mean, the
term making it is so relative.

00:10:32.026 --> 00:10:34.434
- I'm so glad you're jumping
on this, it is probably,

00:10:34.434 --> 00:10:38.534
I believe with anything
that works out for you,

00:10:38.534 --> 00:10:40.392
I don't think it's a sense
of responsibility, but

00:10:40.392 --> 00:10:43.335
I like giving back to
the game that put me on,

00:10:43.335 --> 00:10:46.645
so I love entrepreneurship so much.

00:10:46.645 --> 00:10:49.876
So much of who I am and what
I'm about is to give back

00:10:49.876 --> 00:10:50.709
to the system.

00:10:50.709 --> 00:10:52.200
But you have to update the system.

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:56.242
And we have to update what
being successful looks like,

00:10:56.242 --> 00:10:58.279
it has to be happy, not rich.

00:10:58.279 --> 00:11:00.075
It has to be.

00:11:00.075 --> 00:11:01.499
- [Alli] I mean, I couldn't
agree with that more.

00:11:01.499 --> 00:11:04.119
I mean, money was not
the driver ever for me.

00:11:04.119 --> 00:11:05.900
I mean, money was like gravy.

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:08.309
It was like, oh, we're
gonna actually like,

00:11:08.309 --> 00:11:10.241
make a living, doing this; amazing!

00:11:10.241 --> 00:11:11.932
But, it was never about money.

00:11:11.932 --> 00:11:15.263
And we didn't pay ourselves for years

00:11:15.263 --> 00:11:16.328
when we first started Drybar.

00:11:16.328 --> 00:11:19.170
It was never about money,
it's never been about money.

00:11:19.170 --> 00:11:20.773
- Did you have a family when you started?

00:11:20.773 --> 00:11:22.581
- Yes. I had my two sons,

00:11:22.581 --> 00:11:24.429
and they were three and
five when I started,

00:11:24.429 --> 00:11:25.577
they're 11 and 14 now.

00:11:25.577 --> 00:11:28.179
- [Gary] Let's talk about
that for five seconds.

00:11:28.179 --> 00:11:29.253
This is a subject matter that I've been

00:11:29.253 --> 00:11:31.662
wanting to get more into.

00:11:31.662 --> 00:11:34.190
- Well it's a particularly
interesting subject matter for me

00:11:34.190 --> 00:11:35.467
because I'm going through a divorce.

00:11:35.467 --> 00:11:36.967
But that's public.

00:11:37.975 --> 00:11:39.756
- So, let's put that on
the side for a second.

00:11:39.756 --> 00:11:41.587
- [Alli] So it's been quite a year for me!

00:11:41.587 --> 00:11:44.449
- I respect that, and I'm
sorry to hear that, but

00:11:44.449 --> 00:11:46.449
if you could indulge me,

00:11:47.435 --> 00:11:50.609
I think one of the other
things that I like to do is

00:11:50.609 --> 00:11:51.555
talk about what I know.

00:11:51.555 --> 00:11:53.572
And I like to talk about
things I've lived through.

00:11:53.572 --> 00:11:55.182
I will never have the luxury

00:11:55.182 --> 00:11:57.535
of being an entrepreneurial mom.

00:11:57.535 --> 00:11:59.849
- [Alli] No, that is never gonna happen.

00:11:59.849 --> 00:12:01.618
- That's right, that is not in the cards.

00:12:01.618 --> 00:12:02.599
- [Alli] Do you have kids though?

00:12:02.599 --> 00:12:03.440
- I do.

00:12:03.440 --> 00:12:05.671
- [Alli] Okay. How old are your kids?

00:12:05.671 --> 00:12:08.060
- Nine and six, about.

00:12:08.060 --> 00:12:08.893
- [Alli] Are you sure?

00:12:08.893 --> 00:12:09.726
- Yeah, I am.

00:12:09.726 --> 00:12:10.559
(laughing)

00:12:10.559 --> 00:12:12.112
It's just they're both
already close to 11 and seven.

00:12:12.112 --> 00:12:14.945
Nine and six, almost 11 and seven.

00:12:17.585 --> 00:12:20.286
And I hate when people think
they understand something

00:12:20.286 --> 00:12:21.571
that they'll never be.

00:12:21.571 --> 00:12:23.010
I love when people are like,
I get you, and I'm like

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:24.940
no, you will never be an immigrant.

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:27.267
Like, I get you, nah you're
never gonna be a woman

00:12:27.267 --> 00:12:30.986
or an African American or
a muslism during this time.

00:12:30.986 --> 00:12:33.466
You can be sympathetic,
you can be empathetic,

00:12:33.466 --> 00:12:34.927
but you'll never fully know.

00:12:34.927 --> 00:12:36.382
So I want to take advantage of this.

00:12:36.382 --> 00:12:38.352
'Cause I have a humongous audience,

00:12:38.352 --> 00:12:41.724
I spend a lot of time engaging
with mother entrepreneurs,

00:12:41.724 --> 00:12:44.220
but I never go all the way with my advice,

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:47.555
because I don't want to give
advice that isn't predicated

00:12:47.555 --> 00:12:48.783
on things that I've tasted.

00:12:48.783 --> 00:12:51.412
I have thoughts and theses, we all do.

00:12:51.412 --> 00:12:53.105
But I wanna take advantage of this.

00:12:53.105 --> 00:12:56.773
How much, if any, just right off the top,

00:12:56.773 --> 00:13:00.001
a core question I'd love to
hear your perspective on.

00:13:00.001 --> 00:13:03.084
How much, if any, guilt did you feel?

00:13:04.928 --> 00:13:06.700
- [Alli] Tons. Always so much guilt.

00:13:06.700 --> 00:13:09.656
- This is something I don't
believe a father will ever feel

00:13:09.656 --> 00:13:11.662
anywhere in the same world as a mother.

00:13:11.662 --> 00:13:13.227
- [Alli] Isn't that fucked up? Sorry.

00:13:13.227 --> 00:13:14.060
- [Gary] I don't think so.

00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:14.893
- Can I curse?

00:13:14.893 --> 00:13:15.726
- [Gary] You can curse a lot.

00:13:15.726 --> 00:13:16.559
I don't think it's fucked up.

00:13:16.559 --> 00:13:20.935
- It is! It is because,
I'm on panels all the time,

00:13:20.935 --> 00:13:23.153
and I speak all the time
and people always ask

00:13:23.153 --> 00:13:25.977
that question to women, like
why don't you feel guilty

00:13:25.977 --> 00:13:28.129
as a father when you don't
get to be home with your kids

00:13:28.129 --> 00:13:29.176
as much as you could
'cause you're working?

00:13:29.176 --> 00:13:30.612
- [Gary] 'Cause it's the
propaganda that we were fed.

00:13:30.612 --> 00:13:31.445
- Yeah, that's what I mean!

00:13:31.445 --> 00:13:32.278
- [Gary] That's what I mean.

00:13:32.278 --> 00:13:33.515
- That's what I mean by it's fucked up.

00:13:33.515 --> 00:13:36.476
- I'm curious about
this, I'm curious about

00:13:36.476 --> 00:13:38.903
the process of giving birth.

00:13:38.903 --> 00:13:40.932
Right? 'Cause I will never feel.

00:13:40.932 --> 00:13:42.196
- [Alli] That is not fun.

00:13:42.196 --> 00:13:45.427
- I'm making an assumption,
though I have no idea,

00:13:45.427 --> 00:13:47.543
that there is some sort
of connection point

00:13:47.543 --> 00:13:49.234
that is fundamentally different

00:13:49.234 --> 00:13:52.402
when a human's in-fucking-side
of you, and then as a human,

00:13:52.402 --> 00:13:54.338
versus, it was never inside of me.

00:13:54.338 --> 00:13:58.141
And look, listen, I know
the way I feel about my mom

00:13:58.141 --> 00:13:59.561
versus the way I feel about my dad.

00:13:59.561 --> 00:14:01.451
I love them endlessly both,

00:14:01.451 --> 00:14:04.648
there's some crazy fuckin'
real shit going on there

00:14:04.648 --> 00:14:06.968
with mom that's just fuckin' real!

00:14:06.968 --> 00:14:09.603
Like, I was of inside of her!

00:14:09.603 --> 00:14:11.869
I even lived there for nine months!

00:14:11.869 --> 00:14:12.755
That was my home!

00:14:12.755 --> 00:14:14.385
- [Alli] I would make that
same joke to my ex-husband

00:14:14.385 --> 00:14:16.529
and say like, they're
more mine than yours,

00:14:16.529 --> 00:14:18.201
because they literally
came out of my body.

00:14:18.201 --> 00:14:20.055
- I genuinelly actually believe that.

00:14:20.055 --> 00:14:21.348
- [Alli] He didn't like that.

00:14:21.348 --> 00:14:24.623
- Yeah, yeah, well,
especially given the context.

00:14:24.623 --> 00:14:27.966
But, forget about, should
guys or not guys feel guilt,

00:14:27.966 --> 00:14:30.966
back to the point; it hurts my soul,

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:34.809
'cause entrepreneurship is hard enough.

00:14:34.809 --> 00:14:38.089
It is so difficult, it
is outrageously lonely.

00:14:38.089 --> 00:14:39.232
It is outrageously lonely.

00:14:39.232 --> 00:14:41.913
- [Alli] You have to have a
thick skin for it, no doubt.

00:14:41.913 --> 00:14:43.044
And especially if you're a mom.

00:14:43.044 --> 00:14:43.877
I mean, luckily,

00:14:43.877 --> 00:14:45.837
my mom actually passed
away three years ago,

00:14:45.837 --> 00:14:47.945
but she was there for
the beginning of Drybar,

00:14:47.945 --> 00:14:50.825
and I had my life got
so turned upside down

00:14:50.825 --> 00:14:53.468
in an amazing way, and I
had to travel all the time,

00:14:53.468 --> 00:14:55.932
I had so much great
opportunities coming at me

00:14:55.932 --> 00:14:57.949
and there was so much momentum
and excitement with Drybar,

00:14:57.949 --> 00:14:59.306
and I just traveled tons.

00:14:59.306 --> 00:15:00.629
And luckily my mom was there

00:15:00.629 --> 00:15:03.301
so she was with my kids all the time,

00:15:03.301 --> 00:15:04.559
and the guilt was massive

00:15:04.559 --> 00:15:06.812
but had I had to leave her with a nanny,

00:15:06.812 --> 00:15:09.167
my kids with a nanny,
that would have been...

00:15:09.167 --> 00:15:11.382
God, I don't even know
if I could have done it

00:15:11.382 --> 00:15:12.975
because my kids were so young.

00:15:12.975 --> 00:15:16.248
My mom was there, it was
someone who was family

00:15:16.248 --> 00:15:18.675
and loved them, and so I felt
very lucky in that regard.

00:15:18.675 --> 00:15:19.848
But you know, listen.

00:15:19.848 --> 00:15:21.681
That's not to say,

00:15:21.681 --> 00:15:23.851
and I feel like the world
is so polarizing right now,

00:15:23.851 --> 00:15:25.552
that's not to say if you have a nanny

00:15:25.552 --> 00:15:27.182
and you're an entrepreneur
that's not okay too.

00:15:27.182 --> 00:15:28.015
- I appreciate that.

00:15:28.015 --> 00:15:28.848
- [Alli] That's okay too.

00:15:28.848 --> 00:15:29.681
Whatever works for you.

00:15:29.681 --> 00:15:30.751
- Look, I appreciate that.

00:15:30.751 --> 00:15:33.824
And that's by the way
why I don't feel like,

00:15:33.824 --> 00:15:35.637
some people are like, Gary!

00:15:35.637 --> 00:15:37.784
Inevitably, to your point, polarizing

00:15:37.784 --> 00:15:39.242
and everybody being so hardcore.

00:15:39.242 --> 00:15:41.223
Gary, you should feel
guilt, no I shouldn't.

00:15:41.223 --> 00:15:42.056
You don't know me.

00:15:42.056 --> 00:15:44.306
Like, what about guys whose

00:15:46.622 --> 00:15:49.361
mother has brain cancer when
they start their business?

00:15:49.361 --> 00:15:51.704
Do you feel bad for them now?

00:15:51.704 --> 00:15:56.356
My biggest thing is, you
know nothing about people.

00:15:56.356 --> 00:15:58.403
- And you never know what's
going through somebody's...

00:15:58.403 --> 00:16:01.450
- You can't imagine how
different any news now.

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:02.868
I'm going through a divorce.

00:16:02.868 --> 00:16:05.738
Ten years ago, not that I
had any judgment on that,

00:16:05.738 --> 00:16:08.881
it's funny that my brain would
think about it for a second,

00:16:08.881 --> 00:16:09.714
what does that mean?

00:16:09.714 --> 00:16:13.695
I have become numb because of
how polarizing the world is,

00:16:13.695 --> 00:16:15.678
My hope is this is what's
actually going to happen

00:16:15.678 --> 00:16:18.011
to everybody else, because
my life has been predicated

00:16:18.011 --> 00:16:20.343
on doing actions a little
bit earlier than others,

00:16:20.343 --> 00:16:21.743
and then figuring it out.

00:16:21.743 --> 00:16:25.609
The level of judgment I have
on anybody at this point

00:16:25.609 --> 00:16:27.682
is almost nonexistent.

00:16:27.682 --> 00:16:29.302
I don't know what's going on!

00:16:29.302 --> 00:16:31.263
I know what's going on
with Raghav and I at work!

00:16:31.263 --> 00:16:32.846
- [Alli] To a certain point, right.

00:16:32.846 --> 00:16:34.113
- He may share something with me,

00:16:34.113 --> 00:16:35.983
Jason's shared some stuff on family stuff,

00:16:35.983 --> 00:16:38.600
I've got some context on that,
I got a little something.

00:16:38.600 --> 00:16:41.245
You don't know what's
going on with your spouse,

00:16:41.245 --> 00:16:43.203
you don't know what's going
on with your children,

00:16:43.203 --> 00:16:44.036
you don't know what's going on

00:16:44.036 --> 00:16:45.784
with your parents all the way.

00:16:45.784 --> 00:16:47.419
Nobody knows anybody else all the way.

00:16:47.419 --> 00:16:51.122
- It's so true. I feel
like my mom always said,

00:16:51.122 --> 00:16:53.161
it kinda relates,

00:16:53.161 --> 00:16:55.047
if you have nothing nice
to say, say nothing.

00:16:55.047 --> 00:16:56.009
- [Gary] I'm a buyer.

00:16:56.009 --> 00:16:58.331
- Yeah, it's like you just don't know.

00:16:58.331 --> 00:16:59.533
And I've always felt it,

00:16:59.533 --> 00:17:01.212
you just don't know what
someone else is going through

00:17:01.212 --> 00:17:02.643
at any given moment, so you're right.

00:17:02.643 --> 00:17:04.149
I could not agree with
that statement more.

00:17:04.149 --> 00:17:06.684
- And then all will go
through judgment moments.

00:17:06.684 --> 00:17:08.400
Whether it's a divorce.

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:11.233
- [Alli] But keep 'em to yourself.

00:17:12.823 --> 00:17:14.011
- But you know you're living it

00:17:14.011 --> 00:17:15.369
through your most inner circle,

00:17:15.369 --> 00:17:17.239
and you said, as it gets more public,

00:17:17.239 --> 00:17:19.085
people have judgment who
are watching on Facebook

00:17:19.085 --> 00:17:20.435
who have never heard of you!

00:17:20.435 --> 00:17:23.638
Literally people are watching,
have never heard of you,

00:17:23.638 --> 00:17:26.462
just heard you say it seven minutes ago,

00:17:26.462 --> 00:17:28.722
and whether they typed it or thought it,

00:17:28.722 --> 00:17:29.819
that's what humans do.

00:17:29.819 --> 00:17:30.931
And that's fine, that's natural,

00:17:30.931 --> 00:17:32.335
it's not like I don't have judgment,

00:17:32.335 --> 00:17:34.992
but I think, much like
we started eating better,

00:17:34.992 --> 00:17:39.558
much like we're now doing
better with our mental health.

00:17:39.558 --> 00:17:41.563
- [Alli] Yeah, I've started
doing transcendental meditation

00:17:41.563 --> 00:17:42.773
which is life changing by the way.

00:17:42.773 --> 00:17:46.152
- Good for you, we'll go
over that in a minute.

00:17:46.152 --> 00:17:47.024
- [Alli] This is going to be a

00:17:47.024 --> 00:17:48.426
self help podcast, by the way!

00:17:48.426 --> 00:17:52.258
- We need to start an
anti-judgment process and movement.

00:17:52.258 --> 00:17:53.623
- [Alli] Yeah, I couldn't agree more.

00:17:53.623 --> 00:17:55.270
- My brother's name is AJ, anti-judgment,

00:17:55.270 --> 00:17:56.794
I kinda like this whole thing right now.

00:17:56.794 --> 00:17:58.245
Please put your phone numbers in,

00:17:58.245 --> 00:18:00.611
I wanna get some phone calls in.

00:18:00.611 --> 00:18:03.528
What was the most ridiculously fun,

00:18:05.026 --> 00:18:07.859
exciting moment in the early part?

00:18:08.970 --> 00:18:13.470
Tell me one story, in 2010
you started, in 2011, 2012.

00:18:14.851 --> 00:18:19.662
Give me some story of where
you were like, holy shit.

00:18:19.662 --> 00:18:20.495
- Honestly there's so many.

00:18:20.495 --> 00:18:21.589
- [Gary] I know, I know; I know girl!

00:18:21.589 --> 00:18:23.050
- And it's a great question
which I don't think I've

00:18:23.050 --> 00:18:24.179
really ever been asked that.

00:18:24.179 --> 00:18:26.164
- And I'll tell you why I've
asked it, because I lived it.

00:18:26.164 --> 00:18:27.930
I had so many moments, but
if I was reversed right now

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:30.902
I'd probably say, when
Conan had me on his show,

00:18:30.902 --> 00:18:33.094
when I was doing my wine
blog, nobody from the internet

00:18:33.094 --> 00:18:35.059
was really on T.V. yet,
besides Perez Hilton,

00:18:35.059 --> 00:18:37.487
and it was like this huge moment

00:18:37.487 --> 00:18:40.768
where my brother came from
college, and we watched it.

00:18:40.768 --> 00:18:43.308
That was a moment where
we were like, holy shit.

00:18:43.308 --> 00:18:45.961
Give me one holy shit moment.

00:18:45.961 --> 00:18:47.675
Like did Oprah come in?

00:18:47.675 --> 00:18:49.137
Did somebody mention a magazine?

00:18:49.137 --> 00:18:50.276
Like, what is a holy shamolee?

00:18:50.276 --> 00:18:51.552
- You know Carson Daly?

00:18:51.552 --> 00:18:52.416
- [Gary] Very much so.

00:18:52.416 --> 00:18:54.288
- And he had a radio show for a long time,

00:18:54.288 --> 00:18:55.264
I don't know if he still does.

00:18:55.264 --> 00:18:57.826
And this was really, I think in 2010,

00:18:57.826 --> 00:19:00.774
within a few months of us starting.

00:19:00.774 --> 00:19:02.746
Which reminds me of
Ryan Seacrest had a show

00:19:02.746 --> 00:19:04.744
and Justin Bieber was
talking about Drybar.

00:19:04.744 --> 00:19:06.386
I mean, there's so many of those moments!

00:19:06.386 --> 00:19:08.735
But Ryan Seacrest, we were brand new,

00:19:08.735 --> 00:19:10.221
maybe two or three months.

00:19:10.221 --> 00:19:11.054
- [Gary] Carson Daly?

00:19:11.054 --> 00:19:11.970
- I'm sorry, Carson Daly.

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:14.017
I started getting all these phone calls.

00:19:14.017 --> 00:19:15.835
